+### [Simultaneous Algebraic Reconstruction Technique (SART)](10119-simultaneous-algebraic-reconstruction-technique-sart.md)
+Updates the volume using all rays in a projection concurrently, offering faster convergence and better noise stability than ART.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Iterative; convergence speed depends on the permutation of angles and the condition number of the system. Typically requires a few iterations for convergence [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Low, as it avoids storing the full system matrix, operating directly on projections and volume updates [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Handles missing data and limited angles better than analytical methods when combined with regularization; computationally efficient in memory [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Slower than analytical methods like FBP for simple cases; convergence can be sensitive to noise and the choice of permutation [S1].
-- **When to use:** When memory is limited, or when dealing with incomplete/limited-angle data where regularization can improve results [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** Iterative; convergence speed depends on the angle permutation and data conditioning. Generally requires a few iterations for convergence [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Avoids explicit storage of the large system matrix $M$, storing only the volume and sinogram data [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Handles non-ideal conditions (e.g., limited angles, coarse sampling) better than Fourier-based methods when combined with regularization [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Higher computational cost than Filtered Back Projection (FBP) [S1]. Convergence can be slow if angle ordering is not optimized [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Algebraic Reconstruction Technique was introduced as an alternative to Fourier space reconstruction methods, which require ideal conditions like a continuous range of angles [S1]. It exploits the linear structure of the tomographic problem to solve the inverse problem iteratively, minimizing the quadratic norm of the projection residuals [S1]. It is widely used in materials science and medical imaging where limited angle access or noisy data is common [S1].
+ART is based on the Kaczmarz method for solving linear systems [S1]. It was adapted for tomographic reconstruction to exploit the linear structure of the problem while avoiding the computational burden of writing out the full linear system [S1]. It is widely used in materials science and medical imaging where regularization can improve quality over FBP [S1].
 
